            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> The show method should only require defining a render style per line, not the entire buffer.\r\n\r\nIt *might* but it definitely shouldn't. The design here gives more freedom to users/sources to customize how *all* items to be shown are displayed. For instance, one of the behavior that is possible thanks to this is aligning all visible items (see #1192). It wouldn't have been possible if the interface was only for a single line.",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The show method should only require defining a render style per line, not the entire buffer.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">It <em>might</em> but it definitely shouldn't. The design here gives more freedom to users/sources to customize how <em>all</em> items to be shown are displayed. For instance, one of the behavior that is possible thanks to this is aligning all visible items (see <a class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"7120144\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/discussions/1192\" data-hovercard-type=\"discussion\" data-hovercard-url=\"/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/discussions/1192/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/discussions/1192\">#1192</a>). It wouldn't have been possible if the interface was only for a single line.</p>",
            "bodyText": "The show method should only require defining a render style per line, not the entire buffer.\n\nIt might but it definitely shouldn't. The design here gives more freedom to users/sources to customize how all items to be shown are displayed. For instance, one of the behavior that is possible thanks to this is aligning all visible items (see #1192). It wouldn't have been possible if the interface was only for a single line.",
